22
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:17,440
Our real magic in this labyrinth is to compost all that toxic cold blue light of

23
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:22,240
the modern algorithm and just transform it into living green medicine.

24
00:01:22,240 --> 00:01:24,320
It's a literal turning of our digital shame and

25
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:29,280
burnout into something that can deeply nourish both the soil and the soul.

26
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:32,800
Look at how the concrete just yields to the vibrant soil here.

27
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:36,080
At the very center of our practice, it's a beautifully simple truth.

28
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:40,840
The earth functions as both a kitchen to feed us and a pharmacy to heal us.

29
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:42,960
You really just have to know how to look.

30
00:01:42,960 --> 00:01:45,840
To feel truly alive, we've got to shift our gaze.

31
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:48,440
We can't look at plants as static commodities anymore.

32
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:51,480
They are living, breathing medicine.

33
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:55,040
So, our clock strikes our one, bringing us to Kana.

34
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:59,320
This is the Cilician plant, traditionally known as Kogud, or the Chew Things.

35
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:02,960
Historically, shepherds chewed this to endure long, arid distances.

36
00:02:02,960 --> 00:02:06,800
It's reputed to bring a feeling of just simply being yourself, only happier.

37
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,080
We prepare it by carefully fermenting the plant.

38
00:02:09,080 --> 00:02:10,240
But watch your step.

39
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:12,280
Here we meet our very first Minotaur.

40
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:16,960
You see, in this labyrinth, the Minotaurs are the hazards we absolutely must respect.

41
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,920
For Kana, the beast is psychoactive overuse.

42
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:24,520
We ward it off by treating the plant's history and power with complete, humble respect.

43
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:29,280
As we wander into the second hour, we find Sothelandia, or the Cankerbossy.

44
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:34,040
There is such a beautiful Zulu name for this plant, in Siwa, which means, that which takes

45
00:02:34,040 --> 00:02:35,240
away the dark.

46
00:02:35,240 --> 00:02:40,160
Historically, it's reputed to offer profound comfort, famously used to soothe grieving

47
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:41,280
widows.

48
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:44,600
We draw out its properties through a nice, gentle decoction.

49
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:49,360
The Minotaur lurking at the station, though, it takes the form of toxic lookalikes.

50
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:54,800
We navigate past these false plants by requiring exact, careful botanical identification before

51
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:56,960
a single leaf is ever taken.

52
00:02:56,960 --> 00:03:01,180
We're entering a quiet clearing now to gather the Fineboss Tizans.

53
00:03:01,180 --> 00:03:05,840
This is where our extraction shifts from heavy boiling to a much more peaceful matter.

54
00:03:05,840 --> 00:03:08,640
We simply steep the leaves in hot water.

55
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:14,420
It's a moment to just pause, breathe in that rising, earthy steam, and read the cup.

56
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:18,440
These next three hours are deeply rooted in the mountains of the Cape, offering us

57
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:21,860
a gentle, everyday sort of vitality.

58
00:03:21,860 --> 00:03:25,800
Our three presents Buchu, a classic Cape Tizan.

59
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:30,360
We steep these incredibly aromatic leaves to create a brew historically trusted for soothing

60
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:32,080
warmth and internal comfort.

61
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:35,840
I'm telling you, it truly feels like bringing the very scent of the mountain right into

62
00:03:35,840 --> 00:03:36,840
your kitchen.

63
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:41,000
The beast we face here, however, is the hazard of unsafe, concentrated doses.

64
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,160
Buchu is remarkably potent, so we respect it by adhering strictly to the correct, gentle,

65
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:47,760
traditional dose.

66
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:51,680
Stepping into the fourth hour, we greet an old favorite, Ruybos.

67
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:56,980
This is perhaps our most famous Fineboss export, and it's a beautifully easy-to-grow plant.

68
00:03:56,980 --> 00:04:01,560
We coax out its deep, earthy flavor by steeping the finely cut and fermented needles.

69
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:05,940
Now, while it's a deeply forgiving plant, a Minotaur still guards it.

70
00:04:05,940 --> 00:04:10,660
We have to be entirely certain of our botanical identity out in the wild, confirming we actually

71
00:04:10,660 --> 00:04:15,220
hold true Ruybos, and not some deceptive, wild imposter.

72
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:20,760
To round out our T-zones in the fifth hour, we come upon Honeybush, and oh, just as the

73
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:26,620
name promises, we steep this plant for a traditionally sweet, golden brew that smells just like

74
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:28,120
wild nectar.

75
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:30,680
It is such a deeply comforting cup.

76
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:36,180
But exactly like its cousin Ruybos, the Honeybush's Minotaur is misidentification.

77
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:40,640
We really have to respect the wild landscape, ensuring we never confuse this sweet medicine

78
00:04:40,640 --> 00:04:44,480
with non-medicinal, potentially harmful shrubs up on the mountain.

79
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:48,720
For hour six, our extraction is practically instantaneous.

80
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:51,340
We arrive at Balbine, the burned jelly plant.

81
00:04:51,340 --> 00:04:53,760
This is pure kid, immediate first aid.

82
00:04:53,760 --> 00:04:55,680
There's no brewing required here.

83
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:00,200
You simply snap a plump leaf to extract the raw, soothing jelly and apply it directly

84
